  [1mA. Plain CRUD, add events later.[0m [38;5;153mUPDATE tasks SET ...[39m in place; no history. Simplest possible task layer, and nothing
  is wasted if notifications never ship. But when they do, you retrofit change capture into every write path — or resort
  to database triggers or before/after diffing — and you'll have lost every change that happened before the retrofit.
  [3mComplexity: trivial.[0m

  [1mB. Transactional outbox.[0m Every mutation writes the task row [3mand[0m an append-only [38;5;153mtask_events[39m row ([38;5;153mtask_id[39m, [38;5;153mactor_id[39m,
  [38;5;153mtype[39m, [38;5;153mfield[39m, [38;5;153mold[39m, [38;5;153mnew[39m, [38;5;153moccurred_at[39m) in one transaction. Sub-project 3 becomes a reader of that table rather than a
  rewrite of this one. Costs one extra table and the discipline of a single write path (a thin service layer instead of
  ad-hoc queries in handlers). Gives you an activity feed almost for free, and events can never disagree with state,
  because they commit together. [3mComplexity: moderate.[0m

  [1mC. Event sourcing.[0m The event log is the source of truth; task rows become a projection rebuilt from it. Perfect audit
  fidelity and time-travel. Costs projection code, replay handling, and a much harder mental model for what is
  fundamentally a to-do list. [3mComplexity: high.[0m

  [1mI recommend B.[0m It's the only one that makes notifications an additive change rather than a rewrite, and the marginal
  cost over A is roughly one table plus keeping writes in one place — which a codebase this size should do regardless. C
  buys fidelity you have no stated need for.

  The cost of B that I want to be honest about: it constrains how you write. Every task mutation must go through the
  service layer, and "just run a quick UPDATE in the handler" becomes a bug that silently drops notifications. That's a
  real discipline tax, enforced by tests rather than by the compiler.
